U='https://ftp.mdt.mt.gov/other/webdata/external/const/specifications/2025/SPEC-BOOK-REDLINE.pdf'
S='Montana DOT Standard Specifications for Road and Bridge Construction, 2025 redline book, Sections 603 and 604 (read 2026-11-20)'
add('spec-mt-pipe-1','MT','Montana MDT culverts, storm drains and sewers: 6-inch lifts and 4 feet of cover before heavy equipment (Section 603)',
"Montana (MDT Standard Specifications, 2025 book, Section 603, Culverts, Storm Drains, Sanitary Sewers). Bedding is placed per Section 207 and the Detailed Drawings, with material tamped firmly under the pipe haunches (603.03.2, 603.04.3(B)). Backfill goes in layers of 6 inches loose thickness or less, to the moisture and density required by Subsection 203.03.3 (the percent is set there, not in 603). Heavy equipment stays off until cover is at least 4 feet or one-half the pipe diameter, whichever is greater (603.03.4(A)). Round concrete pipe joints are watertight with rubber gaskets (603.03.3(A)); siphon and irrigation pipe get a water-tightness test (MT 420); manhole connections follow ASTM C923. Section 604, manholes and inlets: backfill in loose layers of 6 inches or less, foundation compacted for full bearing.",S,U,'2025')

U='https://www.dot.state.wy.us/files/live/sites/wydot/files/shared/Construction/2021%20Standard%20Specifications/Wyoming%202021%20Standard%20Specifications%20for%20Road%20and%20Bridge%20Construction.pdf'
S='Wyoming DOT Standard Specifications for Road and Bridge Construction, 2021 edition, Sections 206 and 212 (read 2026-11-20; Section 603 not read)'
add('spec-wy-backfill-1','WY','Wyoming WYDOT culvert excavation and backfill: 8-inch lifts at 95 percent (Section 206)',
"Wyoming (WYDOT 2021 Standard Specifications, Section 206, Excavation and Backfill for Culverts). Backfill in loose layers of about 8 inches, each compacted to at least 95.0 percent of maximum density at moisture between plus 2 and minus 4 percent of optimum (206.4.5.1). Compact the last 6 inches below structures or bedding to at least 95.0 percent (206.4.4). Over water and sewer lines, hand-place the first 12 inches. Do not backfill against cast-in-place boxes until 14 calendar days or 80 percent of design strength. Flowable fill: at least 12 inches over the conduit and no vehicle loads until 24 hours after it sets (206.4.5.3). Pipe bedding and laying are in Section 603 (603.4.4 and 603.4.5). Section 212, structure backfill: loose layers of about 8 inches compacted to the density of the adjacent material, placed evenly on both sides.",S,U,'2021')

U='https://azdot.gov/sites/default/files/2025-03/ADOT-Construction-Manual_Chapter_5_Sections_501-505.pdf'
S='ADOT Construction Manual, Chapter 5, Sections 501-505 (March 2025), describing the ADOT Standard Specifications for Road and Bridge Construction; edition notice at azdot.gov/business/contracts-and-specifications (both read 2026-11-20)'
add('spec-az-pipe-1','AZ','Arizona ADOT pipe culverts and storm drains (Section 501) and the 2027 spec book change',
"Arizona (ADOT Standard Specifications for Road and Bridge Construction). Edition: the 2021 edition is in use now; ADOT says the 2027 edition applies to all projects advertising on and after January 4, 2027, electronic only, so check which book your contract names. Section 501, Pipe Culvert and Storm Drains, as described in ADOT's own Construction Manual (March 2025): a written trenching plan is required for trenches deeper than 5 feet (501-1); trench widths and the minimum 1 foot of cover are on Standard Drawing C-13.15, and cover must be increased for construction traffic, which is the contractor's responsibility (501-3.01); pipe 36 inches and larger in a trench is bedded in cement-treated slurry from the trench bottom to the springline (501-3.04); replacement backfill is compacted to 95 percent of maximum density (501-3.07(B)). The manual advises inspectors that heavy vibratory equipment should not run directly over pipe until there is at least 3 feet of cover and that nothing should be placed on flowable fill for 24 hours. Catch basins (503) and manholes (505) are backfilled in level 8-inch layers. In the Phoenix area, city and county work usually follows the MAG Uniform Standard Specifications instead.",S,U,'2021 (2027 from Jan 4, 2027)','medium')

U='https://apps.fortworthtexas.gov/ProjectResources/ResourcesP/02%20-%20Construction%20Documents/Specifications/Div%2033-Utilities/33%2005%2010_Utility%20Trench%20Excavation%20Embedment%20and%20Backfill.pdf'
S='City of Fort Worth Standard Specification 33 05 10, Utility Trench Excavation, Embedment and Backfill, revised April 2, 2021 (read 2026-11-20)'
add('spec-tx-fortworth-1','TX','Fort Worth, Texas utility trench spec: embedment, 12-inch lifts, 95 and 98 percent, test frequency (33 05 10)',
"Texas, City of Fort Worth (Standard Specification 33 05 10, Utility Trench Excavation, Embedment and Backfill, revised April 2, 2021). Embedment and initial backfill go 6 to 12 inches above the pipe for water lines and for sanitary sewer and HDPE storm pipe; reinforced concrete storm pipe is embedded to the spring line; polypropylene storm pipe gets crushed rock to the top of pipe (3.4.D). Embedment and initial backfill are compacted to 95 percent Standard Proctor (ASTM D698). Final backfill: loose lifts of 12 inches or less; 95 percent under existing or future pavement less than 15 feet deep, 98 percent for select fill 15 feet and deeper, 95 percent outside pavement; moisture from minus 2 to plus 5 percent of optimum (3.4.E.3). Density tests: at least one per 2 vertical feet of compacted backfill and at least every 200 feet of trench, staggered by lift (3.7.A.2.d). Water lines 12 inch and smaller: top of pipe at least 42 inches below proposed grade unless the drawings say otherwise. Trench safety follows OSHA Subpart P and an Excavation Safety Plan designed by a Texas-licensed engineer. Other Dallas-Fort Worth cities use the NCTCOG standard specifications with their own amendments.",S,U,'2021')

U='https://law.cornell.edu/regulations/texas/30-Tex-Admin-Code-SS-217-53'
S='30 Texas Administrative Code 217.53 (TCEQ, wastewater collection pipe design; amended eff. 12/4/2015) and 30 TAC 290.44 (water distribution), read at law.cornell.edu 2026-11-20'
add('spec-tx-separation-1','TX','Texas water and sewer separation: the 9-foot rule (TCEQ 30 TAC 217.53 and 290.44)',
"Texas uses 9 feet, not 10. TCEQ rules: sewer pipe and manholes should be at least 9 feet from water lines, in a separate trench and below the water line where possible (30 TAC 217.53(d); 30 TAC 290.44(e)(1)). When 9 feet cannot be met, parallel lines: the water line at least 2 feet above and 4 feet to the side of the sewer, with 150 psi pressure-rated sewer pipe, or encasement (217.53(d)(6); 290.44(e)(4)(A)). Sewer crossing under a water line: 150 psi pipe with at least 6 inches of separation, one length at least 18 feet long centered on the water line; or other pipe with at least 2 feet of separation and cement-stabilized sand; or a casing pipe at least two sizes larger with spacers no more than 5 feet apart (217.53(d)(7)). Cement-stabilized sand under 217.53 has at least 160 lb of cement per cubic yard; under 290.44 at least 10 percent cement (2.5 bags per yard). A manhole within 9 feet of a water line must pass the leak test or get at least 1 foot of cement-stabilized sand. Water lines: at least 24 inches of cover, 35 psi minimum pressure, fire hydrants not within 9 feet of sewer lines, new mains disinfected per AWWA C651 with at least one bacteria sample per 1,000 feet (290.44). Gravity sewers are at least 6 inches in diameter and designed for at least 2 feet per second (217.53).",S,U,'2015 amendment')

U='https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/florida/Fla-Admin-Code-Ann-R-62-555-314'
S='Florida Administrative Code Rule 62-555.314, Location of Public Water System Mains (amended eff. 2/26/2025), read at law.cornell.edu 2026-11-20'
add('spec-fl-separation-1','FL','Florida water main separation from sewer, storm and reclaimed lines (Rule 62-555.314)',
"Florida has its own separation numbers (F.A.C. Rule 62-555.314, amended effective 2/26/2025). Side by side, a new or relocated water main is at least: 3 feet from storm sewers, stormwater force mains and regulated reclaimed water lines; 3 feet, preferably 10, from vacuum sanitary sewers; 6 feet, preferably 10, from gravity or pressure sanitary sewers and wastewater force mains (gravity sewer can come down to 3 feet if the bottom of the water main is at least 6 inches above the top of the sewer); 10 feet from onsite sewage (septic) systems. Crossing a gravity or vacuum sewer or storm sewer: water main at least 6 inches, preferably 12, above, or at least 12 inches below; above is preferred. Crossing a pressure sewer, force main or reclaimed line: at least 12 inches above or below. At crossings, center one full length of water main, or keep water main joints at least 3 feet from storm and vacuum sewer joints and 6 feet from sanitary sewer and force main joints. No water main may pass through or touch a sanitary sewer manhole. Hydrant drains keep the same distances. The Department can allow exceptions with extra protection such as pressure-rated sewer pipe, restrained joints, a watertight casing or 4 inches of concrete encasement.",S,U,'2025 amendment')
add('spec-fl-edition-1','FL','Florida FDOT Standard Specifications: which edition is in force (July-to-June books)',
"Florida (FDOT Standard Specifications for Road and Bridge Construction). FDOT publishes a new eBook each fiscal year: the FY 2026-27 book took effect July 1, 2026 and the FY 2027-28 book takes effect July 1, 2027; your contract names the one that applies. The sections a pipe crew lives in are Section 125 (Excavation for Structures and Pipe), Section 430 (Pipe Culverts) and Section 425 (Inlets, Manholes, and Junction Boxes), with jack and bore in Section 556. The numbers in those sections were not read for this library (the files would not open here), so for lift thickness, density and pipe inspection go to the book itself at fdot.gov/specifications. County and city utility work in Florida usually follows the utility's own standards, and water main separation follows state Rule 62-555.314.",'FDOT Standard Specification Library, fdot.gov/specifications/standard-specification-library (edition list read 2026-11-20); section numbers not re-read','https://www.fdot.gov/specifications/standard-specification-library','FY 2026-27','medium')

U='https://www.in.gov/dot/div/contracts/standards/rsp/sep25/700/715-R-809 260901.pdf'
S='INDOT Recurring Special Provision 715-R-809, Pipe Culverts, and Storm and Sanitary Sewers, dated 09-01-26 (read 2026-11-20)'
add('spec-in-pipe-1','IN','Indiana INDOT pipe: mandrel testing of plastic pipe, Section 715',
"Indiana (INDOT Standard Specifications Section 715, Pipe Culverts, and Storm and Sanitary Sewers, as changed by Recurring Special Provision 715-R-809 dated 09-01-26). After the visual or video inspection, plastic pipe is mandrel tested: corrugated and smooth-wall polyethylene, corrugated polypropylene, and profile and smooth-wall PVC. The Engineer picks the runs, and at least 10 percent of the total length of each material is tested; underdrain pipe is exempt. Backfill for corrugated pipe may not have particles larger than half the corrugation depth. Thermoplastic and metal pipe must come from INDOT's qualified products list. The deflection limit, lift thickness and compaction numbers are in the base Section 715 and Section 211 text, which was not read here.",S,U,'2026 RSP','medium')
